{
  "0": "Interest aggregation: in all four datasets, replace the existing readout with a normalized recency-weighted mean of the four most recent valid GRU states, using weights 1, 2, 4, 8 from oldest to newest. At each training target, pool only preceding states and exclude padding. This may smooth noisy final states while retaining recent intent, with negligible added computation. Preserve history selection, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer; mirror the readout in the predictor writer.",
  "1": "Training strategy: in all four datasets, multiply the current learning rate by 0.7 while preserving the optimizer type and every other training setting. Smaller updates may reduce the validation oscillation visible in the supplied trace and improve generalization, especially for Electronics. Keep preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, the 200-epoch cap, stopping rules, batch size, architecture, features, readout, scoring and loss unchanged.",
  "2": "Interaction mechanism: in each category's single network, use diagonal bilinear compatibility s(h,i)=sum_d a_d*h_d*e_i,d, retaining any existing item bias. Initialize the jointly trained per-dimension coefficients a to one. Learning which recurrent dimensions matter for item compatibility may improve ranking with only O(hidden_size) additional parameters. Preserve the GRU backbone, input features, history readout, loss and optimizer, and update the predictor writer to load and apply a.",
  "3": "Feature construction: in all four datasets, scale each existing input item embedding by 1+0.1*min(c,3), where c counts that item's earlier occurrences within the supplied causal history. Compute c before the current interaction, excluding padding and target/future interactions; retain the original history selection. This deterministic repeat indicator may distinguish recurring preferences from one-off purchases. Keep learned embedding tables, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed; apply identical feature construction in prediction."
}
